speechtext:
The capital cost will be a fixed sum, whatever the total cost will be, and it will not bear any interest developing from year to year to increase that amount. Is that correct? I ask the minister this because he knows that, for instance, the Jacques Cartier bridge is an example of a government enterprise where not only did the income not meet the operating costs for many years but also it did not come close to meeting the interest charges upon the debt, and so instead of the amortization on the Jacques Cartier bridge
[Mr. Fisher.}
fading away over the years it has been piling up. I would assume that that would not be so in this particular case.
